WebPilot MCP Server — Dual-Use Disclosure
==========================================

This package is published under npm's Dual-Use Content Policy
(https://docs.npmjs.com/policies/dual-use/) and is declared as
`"contentPolicy": { "class": "dual-use" }` in package.json.

The declaration is not an accusation. It is context for automated and human
reviewers, because the capabilities below legitimately resemble the behaviour
of malware to a scanner.


1. What this package does
-------------------------

WebPilot is a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server that lets an AI coding agent
drive a real, user-authenticated Chrome browser through a companion Chrome
extension. It exposes browser automation primitives: navigation, clicking,
typing, form filling, page snapshots, text/table extraction, page-script
evaluation, and task/workflow automation.


2. Why these capabilities may look malicious to an automated scanner
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- It executes caller-supplied JavaScript in the context of arbitrary web pages.
- It drives a browser that already holds the user's logged-in sessions, so it
  inherits whatever cookies and tokens that profile contains.
- It installs a browser extension and runs a postinstall script on the
  developer's machine.
- It can navigate to any http(s) URL, extract page content, and issue
  authenticated requests through the browser session.


3. Legitimate use
-----------------

- Agent-driven web testing and regression testing of web applications.
- Reading and extracting content from pages the operator can already reach,
  including internal tools they are logged into.
- Reproducing a bug report by driving the affected UI the way the reporter does.

Every operation runs locally on the developer's own machine, against a browser
the developer already controls. The package contains no obfuscation, does not
fetch or execute code from a remote origin at runtime, and contains no
fixtures with real account data.


4. Network, filesystem, and process access
------------------------------------------

- Network: a local loopback WebSocket bridge between this server and the
  Chrome extension, plus the web pages the operator explicitly navigates to.
- Filesystem: reads only its own package files. The postinstall script prints
  setup instructions and performs no file, network, or process mutation.
- Processes: runs a local Node.js MCP server on stdio. It does not enumerate,
  inject into, or spawn unrelated processes.


5. Permissions required
-----------------------

- A Chrome/Chromium extension that the user loads in developer mode.
- The user installs and runs the package themselves; it is not a transitive
  dependency of other packages.
- Browser access is limited to the tabs the user has open or that the agent
  opens on their behalf.


6. What users must not do with it
---------------------------------

- Do not point it at systems you are not authorized to access.
- Do not use it to collect, exfiltrate, or share other people's credentials or
  sessions.
- Do not use the page-script evaluation facility outside the scope of the task
  the user actually asked for.


7. Review aids
--------------

- Source repository: https://github.com/openpeng/webdoc
- The published tarball contains no generated binaries, no bundled
  credentials, and no fixtures containing real account data.
- The extension client is shipped unmodified under `browser-extension/`, so the
  full client-side behaviour is reviewable alongside the server code.
- The published tarball's shasum is recorded in the release commit for
  `webpilot-mcp-server` v1.1.1.

